{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/curseborne/1539917-wicked-success-and-cruel-failure","id":1539917,"title":"Wicked Success and Cruel Failure","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Curseborne"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-08-06T00:16:32-07:00","id":1539917,"title":"Wicked Success and Cruel Failure","content":"I ran a session for the Curseborne Ashcan and I'm a little confused about how Wicked Successes and Cruel Failures work. I understand how they work mechanically, but how do you use them in game? From the examples given they seem like they're meant to be big, important, rare, long lasting things, but they seem to happen an awful lot, especially with Wicked Successes. I'm assuming/hoping the full book explains them better, but how many times can &quot;An otherworldly entity arrive to assist or to congratulate you.&quot; because they were so amazed by how you picked that simple lock. Is &quot;An enemy or rival takes note of the character\u2019s failure and gloats.&quot; really a cruel failure? What does it mean exactly when &quot;An implosion of power causes trouble.&quot;? I thought having curse dice was meant to be bad, or had a greater impact on the dice roll, but as it is Wicked Successes and Cruel Failures are just kind of strange and awkward and it really doesn't seem worth the effort.<br /> <br /> I think it would be better if curse dice just worked more like hunger dice from v5. Wicked Successes = you succeed but with additional complications. Cruel Failures = you fail messily and spectacularly. Maybe even have it trigger a Torment or something.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":13542,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13542-shadowdragon","user":"Shadowdragon","date":". 08-06-2024, 03:40 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13542-shadowdragon","name":"Shadowdragon","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"205"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-06T06:07:17-07:00","id":1539921,"title":"","content":"To my knowledge, Curseborne doesn\u2019t have a mechanic to let you put off the effects of curse dice the way v5 has willpower which will let you reroll to prevent a Messy Crit or a Bestial Failure.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25049-zakrulz","name":"ZakRulz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25049_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"238"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-06T07:36:12-07:00","id":1539922,"title":"","content":"The blog post about Curse Dice (<a href=\"https://theonyxpath.com/curseborne-curse-dice/\" target=\"_blank\">https://theonyxpath.com/curseborne-curse-dice/</a>) has a bit about wicked success and cruel failures. Quoted here for convenience.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Running and Roleplaying Curses</b><br /> <br /> <br /> Curses are intended to help reflect a type of horror genre where you are the monster, and you can\u2019t control your monstrous nature. There\u2019s no power without consequence, there\u2019s no gain without risk, and there\u2019s no getting away from your cursed existence.<br /> <br /> That said, we don\u2019t want games of <b>Curseborne </b>to feel oppressive. Wicked successes and cruel failures are ways to reflect the curse\u2019s hold on a character, but ultimately, it\u2019s up to the Storyguide to determine how much of an effect either one has on the current narrative. If they come up several times in a row, it might feel daunting to suddenly be dealing with 5 different twists and turns within the narrative.<br /> <br /> I like to consider some things called escalations when thinking about how to handle wicked successes and cruel failures. You do not need to introduce an entirely new element to a scene, or come up with a shocking twist. Instead, escalate already present action. In a combat scene, maybe an enemy turns on one of their own allies for an indiscernible reason. Figuring that out might be the thread of your next story, or the players may simply accept the welcome reprieve. In a social scene, maybe a character that went from being relatively neutral towards the characters suddenly becomes frightened. Maybe they get a phone call and need to leave. Maybe a fight breaks out in the bar they\u2019re talking in, and they all need to leave or risk getting sucked into it.<br /> <br /> If you escalate the already present action, then you simply make things a little better, a little worse, or a little more complicated without having to come up with an entirely new story seed or scenario. </div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Lead Writer for the Savage Age of Werewolf: the Apocalypse: <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/428750/Ghost-Lineages-The-Advanced-Players-Guide-to-the-Savage-Age\" target=\"_blank\">Ghost Lineages</a>, <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/312700/The-Rise-of-the-Garou-The-Savage-Age-Volume-Two\" target=\"_blank\">Rise of the Garou</a>, <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/331302/Tools-of-Extinction-The-Savage-Age-Volume-Three\" target=\"_blank\">Tools of Extinction</a>, <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/352717/Tribebook-Wlewa\" target=\"_blank\">Tribebook: Wlewa</a>.<br /> <br /> Want RPGs in libraries? Check out my book, <a href=\"http://www.abc-clio.com/ABC-CLIOCorporate/product.aspx?pc=A3951P\" target=\"_blank\">Dragons in the Stacks</a>. Now with <a href=\"http://www.ennie-awards.com/blog/2015-ennie-award-nominees-and-spotlight-winners/\" target=\"_blank\">awards</a>!<br /> \u200b","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1813-grimjaws","name":"grimjaws","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1813_4.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"134"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-06T08:20:03-07:00","id":1539925,"title":"","content":"If they work like Hunger Dice I'll lose all interest in it. Hunger Dice are one of the worst choices I've seen in a game for my tastes. Cursed Dice working like that would be a worse implementation of the idea because their position in the system is reversed. Hunger Dice aren't something you want to accrue, they don't benefit you. They're a result of failing a check to use powers. Curse Dice are the fuel for your powers and are things you are therefor after. Additionally having no Curse Dice triggers your Damnation so it all just ends up as a cascading array of ways to wrench control from the player's hands to introduce additional complications. Altered Successes are just Exceptional/Dramatic from CofD right now but need a little fine tuning in how that applies.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27505-dragox27","name":"Dragox27","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27505_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-07T03:23:22-07:00","id":1539982,"title":"","content":"The problem with hunger dice is that it is a very wild experience, not for its results, but because it may matter nothing for 3 sessions just to become a monster in the next, without adding a dice to the pool.<br /> <br /> it has the weird dependence in how often you roll the dice, my first vampire players used to roll all the time, for every action, later I start playing with another team, and it was all about narrative, so we rolled 2 or 3 times in a session, except combat. To be fair, I never played V5, but the idea of hunger dice may be a interesting idea if played the exact way it was intended, with the correct frequency of rolls, but may become weird quite fast if not.<br /> <br /> I had a similar problem with Alien and the fear/stress dice. In one session people built stress slowly, and in the next it went crazy, with people running away from the scene before it even start properly.<br /> <br /> Curse dice seam to be more like energy pools with a little side effect, and less hunger dice.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/en/product/481584/house-rules-bundle-bundle\" target=\"_blank\">House Rules - The Bundle</a> - House Rules for Everyone<br /> <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/en/product/481581/powered-inspiration-bundle\" target=\"_blank\">Powered Inspired</a> - Powered Characters using Inspiration and Gifts<br /> <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/413362/Fists-and-Tomes-BUNDLE\" target=\"_blank\">Fists and Tomes</a> - Inspired Martial Arts and Mysticism for Talents<br /> <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/en/product/492147/supertech\" target=\"_blank\">Supertech</a> - Super Science in the hands of Talents","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24985-mateus-luz","name":"Mateus Luz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24985_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2950"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}